@incollection{laird_botanical_1999,
	title = {The Botanical Medicine Industry},
	isbn = {978-0-429-34154-0},
	abstract = {This chapter focuses on herbal or botanical medicines, sold in a variety of forms and reflecting differing levels of processing. It examines nutraceuticals, which are a rapidly growing market, with demand for access to new natural products. Botanical medicines, as distinct from pharmaceuticals, are produced directly from whole plant material. Top-selling species used in commercial botanical medicine products vary by country and region. The structure of the botanical medicine industry is complex and in flux, moving towards greater globalisation and consolidation of companies. Manufacturing and marketing companies produce private-label products, which they market for retail sale. Traditional knowledge is widely used in the botanical medicine industry as the basis for determining safety and efficacy, to develop agronomic practices for cultivation of materials, and to guide the development of new products. The regulatory environment for botanical medicines varies a great deal by country. In Europe and Japan botanical medicines are comprehensively regulated, with standards set for both manufacturing and ingredients.},
